  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 \n\nThe rain has push out overnight and ridging builds into the \narea today with dry and warmer conditions. Mostly cloudy skies \nwill likely linger into the afternoon, however high temperatures\nare still expected to climb into the mid to upper 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nThe pattern will remain active this week with the system\nimpacting the region on Tuesday. A low pressure system is \nforecast to track eastward across the Great Lakes into Ontario \nas the day progresses. Warm SW flow out ahead of this system\nwill push high temperatures well into the 70s by Tuesday\nafternoon with upper 70s possible west of the I81 corridor. \nThis will allow for instability to increase out ahead of the \napproaching cold front, producing a chance for strong \nthunderstorms west of I-81. Current indices look decent for mid\nOCtober with CAPE of 750-1200 J/kg, and 35-40kts of 0-6km bulk \nshear. However, uncertainty for thunderstorm coverage and\nintensity is still high at this time, as the models continue to\nhave differences in timing and location of the low. \n\nBehind this system, high pressure is expected to build back in,\nbut with zonal flow aloft expected through the week. This will\nallow for potential a few weak short waves to bring more chances\nfor isolated showers Wednesday and possibly Thursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3\n\nOur next big system looks to be Thursday night through Friday,\nfollowed by a deep upper trough settling over the Northeast U.S.\nthis weekend. Widespread rain showers will be possible again\nThursday night through Friday as a fast moving low pressure\nsystem crosses the region. This system will be followed by a\ndeep upper trough/low diving into the area from central Canada.\n\nThis trough has been modeled somewhat consistently between most\nof the major global models, so confidence continues to increase\nfor unsettled conditions this weekend. T850 could dip as low as\n-10C over Central NY on Saturday as cold air advection takes\n hold on NW flow. Lake America water temperatures are quite\n warm, ranging in the upper 50s to low 60s. Such a large \n difference in air and water temperatures should produce a \n decent lake response and our first lake effect snow event of \n the season is possible if this forecast pattern takes shape. \n\n This will certainly be something to watch over the coming days,\n especially because it is very early in the season. Any snow \n accumulation this early is rare, but with trees still mostly \n covered in leaves, any snowfall greater than a couple of inches\n could cause major issues. This is still 6 days away, so changes\nin the forecast are likely over the coming days and the details\ncan be more fine-tuned in the coming days.",
